## Usage

```bash
npm i @pulumi/pulumi @pulumi/aws @pulumi/random

pulumi stack init dev
pulumi config set --secret app-platform:dbPassword "$(openssl rand -base64 24)"
pulumi up -s dev

pulumi stack init prod
pulumi config set --secret app-platform:dbPassword "$(openssl rand -base64 32)"
pulumi up -s prod
```

## How the acceptance criteria are met

| Criterion | Where |
|---|---|
| Bucket versioning + SSE + no public access | `BucketVersioningV2`, `BucketServerSideEncryptionConfigurationV2` (AES256 dev / KMS CMK prod), `BucketPublicAccessBlock`, TLS-only bucket policy |
| RDS deletion protection, backups ≥ 7d, not public | `deletionProtection: true`, `backupRetentionPeriod` validated ≥ 7 in `config.ts`, `publiclyAccessible: false` |
| Secret never plaintext in Git | `pulumi config set --secret` → ciphertext only in `Pulumi.<stack>.yaml`; connection string wrapped in `pulumi.secret()` and stored in Secrets Manager |
| Least privilege IAM | IRSA trust scoped to one `system:serviceaccount:ns:name`; policies enumerate explicit actions, scoped to this bucket/secret/key ARN — no `Action: "*"` |
| Env isolation | Separate stacks (`dev`/`prod`) = separate state files; `pulumi up -s prod` cannot touch dev state |
| Prod hardening | KMS CMKs with rotation, multi-AZ, Performance Insights, `protect: true`, cross-region replication to `us-west-2` |

**Out of scope here** (per spec, but noted): the EKS node groups and the CloudFront/ACM HTTPS front door would be sibling components consuming `serviceRoleArn` and `bucketName` — the `oidcProviderArn` config value is the seam where the cluster component plugs in.
